<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Belgrade</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Belgrade is a city at the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> rivers, now the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. A key frontier fortress controlling the route into central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, it was conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a> in 1521. The capture opened the way for the Ottoman victory at Mohács in 1526 and later campaigns against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/belgrade.jpg" alt="Belgrade" width="320" height="177" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ZlatanJovanovic · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panorama_Belgrad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Bayan I they besieged and took the Byzantine fortress of Sirmium in 582 after a long blockade, then captured <b>Singidunum</b> and raided deep into the Balkans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Eugene shared victory at Blenheim in 1704 and took <b>Belgrade</b> in 1717.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>John Hunyadi, voivode of Transylvania and later governor of the kingdom, won victories in the Long Campaign of 1443–1444 and lost the Second Battle of Kosovo in 1448, but in July 1456 broke the Ottoman siege of <b>Belgrade</b> with the preacher John of Capistrano.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>On 1 December 1918 the Prince Regent Alexander proclaimed the Kingdom of Serbs, Croats and Slovenes in <b>Belgrade</b>, with his father Peter I as king.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The reign of Suleiman the Magnificent (r. 1520–1566) produced its most ambitious campaigns: the capture of <b>Belgrade</b> in 1521, the destruction of the Hungarian royal army at the Battle of Mohács in 1526, the first siege of Vienna in 1529, and the naval operations of Hayreddin Barbarossa, whose…</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Nehru India was a leader of decolonisation and, with Yugoslavia, Egypt and Indonesia, of the Non-Aligned Movement founded at <b>Belgrade</b> in 1961, and Indian troops served widely in United Nations peacekeeping missions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
